<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #a7d9ed; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shopping at the Market – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Is the Pineapple?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Comprehend basic facts in simple educational content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend basic facts about quantities in market settings (e.g., 'How many apples?' 'Three apples, please.').<br>Can identify key details in talks about prices and items at the market (e.g., 'The apples are one dollar each.').<br>Can discuss main ideas from simple audio lessons on shopping (e.g., 'We buy yucas (cassava) and bread at the market.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks, "Where do we buy food?" (Elicit "market" or "store"). The teacher shows flashcards of market items: pineapple, potatoes, cassava, money, shopping list. Students repeat the names chorally. The teacher then displays a picture of a busy market and asks, "What do you see?" Students point to items they recognize from flashcards. The teacher plays a short, upbeat market sound clip (e.g., chatter, vendor calls) and asks, "What sounds do you hear?" to set the scene.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a pineapple and clearly says "pineapple." The teacher points to a picture of money and says "money." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary, emphasizing /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money'. For numbers, the teacher holds up 1, 2, 3 fingers and says "one," "two," "three," clearly articulating the sounds and blending. The teacher models the question "How much is the pineapple?" and the answer "It costs one dollar." using clear intonation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of a pineapple: "Is this a banana or a pineapple?" (Expecting "A pineapple!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a dollar bill: "Is this a toy or money?" (Expecting "Money!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between a customer and a vendor at a market. The dialogue will feature basic questions about items, quantities, and prices, such as: "Excuse me, how much is the pineapple?" "It costs two dollars." "I need five potatoes, please." "Okay, five potatoes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Comprehend simple factual information presented orally in short, clear statements within a familiar context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and then answer, "Where are the people?" (At the market.) "What are they doing?" (Buying/selling food.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (True/False):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with simple True/False statements. Students listen to the audio again and mark T or F.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "The woman wants bananas." (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "The pineapple costs one dollar." (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "They talk about potatoes." (T)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listen (Information Matching):</b> The teacher provides pictures of a pineapple, potatoes, and cassava with spaces next to them. Students listen one more time and draw the correct number (e.g., '2' next to pineapple, '5' next to potatoes) based on the audio.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of WH-questions ('How much is it?', 'How many...?'), Quantities (numbers 1-100), Present Simple for needs/wants. Vocabulary: market items (pineapple, potatoes, cassava), price, cost, money, buy, need, want.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listen and Complete (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher provides a simplified transcript of parts of the dialogue with blanks for numbers or key items. Students listen to specific short segments of the audio again (focused listening) and fill in the missing words or numbers. For example: "I need ______ potatoes." "The pineapple costs ______ dollars." "Do you want ______ cassava?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Listen and Choose (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays a series of flashcards for items (e.g., 3 apples, 5 apples) or prices (e.g., $1, $2). The teacher plays short audio clips (e.g., "I want three apples." or "It costs two dollars."). Students listen and point to the correct flashcard or circle the correct option on their worksheet. This activity reinforces phonemic awareness of numbers like "one," "two," "three," and blends like /tw/ in 'twenty'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Listen and Draw (Pair interaction/Peer check):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short audio clips of a shopper's request (e.g., "I want two pineapples and four potatoes."). Each student listens and draws the requested items and quantities on their individual "shopping list" paper. After listening, they compare their drawings with their partner to check if they both heard and drew the same items and quantities correctly, discussing any discrepancies in English (e.g., "I drew two pineapples. You drew three!"). This focuses on listening for specific details and peer checking listening accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Market Order Fulfilment" (Listening-focused Role-Play). Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group has a set of flashcards representing different market items (pineapple, potatoes, cassava, etc.) and number cards (1-10). One student is the "Cashier" (facilitator), and the others are "Market Vendors."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays pre-recorded audio "customer orders" (e.g., "Hello, I need three pineapples and five potatoes. How much is the cassava?" "Excuse me, I want one big pineapple and ten small potatoes.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Market Vendors" in each group listen carefully to the audio order.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, they quickly identify and gather the correct flashcards (items and quantities) from their pile.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Cashier" in each group checks if the vendors heard the order correctly and gathered the right items/quantities. The cashier might prompt with "How many potatoes did you hear?" if there's a discrepancy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then share with the class what items they heard and gathered. This allows students to actively apply their listening comprehension skills in a dynamic, collaborative shopping scenario, focusing on discerning details like items and exact quantities (numbers 1-100).</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Market Order Fulfilment" task, focusing on individual student's ability to identify items and quantities from audio input.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a final short audio clip (2-3 sentences) describing a simple shopping list (e.g., "I need two pineapples and six cassava. The pineapple costs three dollars each."). Students are given a worksheet with a picture of a blank shopping list. They listen and write down the items and quantities they heard, and then the price for one item. This assesses their ability to comprehend basic facts about quantities and prices from spoken language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main items from the audio (e.g., pineapple, cassava).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies correct quantities for each item (e.g., 'two,' 'six').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the price mentioned for the item (e.g., 'three dollars').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. I can understand many words about shopping (Yes/A little/No).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I can hear numbers well when people talk about prices (Yes/A little/No).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-6 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was easy to hear and understand today? (e.g., "pineapple," "one dollar")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What was difficult to hear or understand today? (e.g., "many numbers," "long sentences")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students were highly engaged with the audio activities, especially the "Listen and Draw" and "Market Order Fulfilment" tasks. Their ability to distinguish quantities and identify items from spoken language was generally good, showing good progress for A1.2. The pronunciation focus on /p/ and /m/ was well-received during modeling. Some students struggled with differentiating between higher numbers (e.g., 'thirteen' vs. 'thirty'), indicating a need for more targeted practice with number phonemics. For the next lesson, I will provide clear visual support for numbers and prices to aid reading comprehension of similar vocabulary and structures. Scaffolds for next lesson will include pre-teaching specific market item names from the listening activity visually before introducing them in a reading text, and providing a glossary for challenging words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Today, we listened to people buying and selling at the market. Tomorrow, in Lesson 2, we will read a short text about a shopping list and prices at the market, using many of the same words like 'pineapple,' 'potatoes,' and 'money,' to understand how they look in writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>